{"name":"Moth’s Wing","hex":"#edf1db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any flying insect of the order Lepidoptera not in the superfamily Papilionoidea, most species of which are nocturnal and can be distinguished from butterflies by feather-like antennae.","Anything that gradually and silently eats, consumes, or wastes any other thing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt for moths."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moth"},{"term":"wing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An appendage of an animal's (bird, bat, insect) body that enables it to fly.","A fin at the side of a ray or similar fish."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To injure slightly (as with a gunshot), especially in the wing or arm.","To fly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"},{"title":"Moth","blocks":[{"text":"Moths are a group of winged insects of the order Lepidoptera, the other well-known members of which are butterflies; they are characterized by dark or dull patterned wings. They were previously classified as suborder Heterocera, but the group is paraphyletic with respect to butterflies and neither subordinate taxon is used in modern classifications. Moths make up the vast majority of the order. There are approximately 160,000 species of moth, many of which have yet to be described. Most species of moth are nocturnal, although there are also crepuscular and diurnal species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Autographa_gamma_en_Trachelospermum_jasminoides_-_02.jpg/330px-Autographa_gamma_en_Trachelospermum_jasminoides_-_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moth","wikidata":"Q1725788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/500px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/1280px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/500px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Condensation in the low-pressure region over the wing of an Airbus A340, passing through humid air","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloud_over_A340_wing.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/500px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/1280px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/500px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The wing of a landing BMI Airbus A319-100. The slats at its leading edge and the flaps